 The sum of the interior angles of an $n$-sided convex polygon is $(n-2) \times 180^\circ$. If the polygon is convex, then all angles are less than $180^\circ$.
An interior right angle is exactly $90^\circ$. Taking enough of those will add up to $180^\circ$, just enough to make the polygon still convex. After that we reached $360^\circ$, which is where we enter a non-convex regular polygon, so no right angles can follow it.
If all the interior angles except for one are right angles, the octagon is not regular, so it has $7$ right angles. This would be achievable if we took two pairs of the octagon's sides to be parallel, with $180^\circ - 90^\circ = 90^\circ$ angles at each of the fourth vertices. Both pairs must be parallel, so the only thing left to do is to arrange them to create two right angles, $360=180+180$, at one of the already-existing vertices.
